A||N||6||Formula88||EGR Tubes sukbstitutes?||||5||
Z||000000||Formula88||02-27-2001||09:16 AM||||So someone on ebay is selling an EGR tube from a Pontiac 6000 2.8V6 and saying it will fit the Fiero if you slot the bolt holes that attach to the EGR valve. <BR> <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=563821678&r=0&t=0&showTutorial=0&ed=983592418&indexURL=0&rd=1" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=563821678&r=0&t=0&showTutorial=0&ed=983592418&indexURL=0&rd=1</A> <P>It's also supposed to be a heavier duty tube than the Fiero tube, and since it's only bid up to about $10 last time I checked, they may be cheaper to buy outright from a parts store.  Has anyone checked this out or heard of it before?  I also was examining mine (broken) and it looks just like flared pipe with a flange on the end.  If that's the case, why not just buy a new length of pipe, put the original flanges on it and flare the tips?  Am I missing something here?  Surely someone has tried this before.  Any success or failure stories?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||02-27-2001||09:42 AM||||I've heard that the 2.8 Firebird EGR tube will fit, also if you slot the holes. The 6000 one is probably very similar. Don't know about using pipe and a flaring tool. Seems like the EGR tube has to flex, at least a little bit. I'm guessing to make up for expansion/contraction with temperature changes.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||02-27-2001||10:08 AM||||That sounds right, since the ad says the 6000 tube should fit a Firebird w/o mods.  I may go check the auto parts store for one later and see how much they are.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mshill||02-27-2001||01:12 PM||||I generally get these (if needed) from the Pick-n-Pull for $5-$6.  each time I go out I try to find one or two just to have a supply.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||02-27-2001||05:47 PM||||someone, i forget who, PISA or FieroStore maybe, have a braided stainless steel one for $100. I think thats the way to go. I replaced mine with no modification from 2.8 Chevy S-10 pu. Next time itll be the stainless. and yes they flex, thats why they crack so often.||||reg||1
